      text: "A dwarf tt plant makes too little GA but has functional GA receptors. Applied GA restores stem elongation. What outcome is expected?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Tall phenotype; homozygous recessive genotype." }, { key: "b", text: "Tall phenotype; homozygous dominant genotype." }, { key: "c", text: "Dwarf phenotype; heterozygous genotype." }, { key: "d", text: "Dwarf phenotype; homozygous dominant genotype." }],
      answer: "a",
      explanation: "Supplying the missing hormone can restore elongation in a GA-biosynthesis-deficient plant that can still perceive GA. The treatment changes the expressed growth phenotype but does not rewrite the alleles, so tt remains tt. The receptor condition is important: some dwarf mutants fail to respond to GA, and the source's rescue diagram is not valid for every genetic cause of dwarfness.",
      sectionId: "gibberellin-growth-germination-and-flowering", source: { document: "biology", page: 302 }, cognitive: "application"
